# lsdp-static player list.
#
# A WARNING BEFORE THE FORMAT: everything below is a hand-kept copy of something
# that lives on the players themselves -- address, port, name, model, firmware
# version. Nothing keeps it in step. A firmware update, a renamed room, a new or
# sold player, or a DHCP lease that moves all leave this file describing a
# network that no longer exists, and nothing will tell you. Worse, the real
# players answer the same queries under the same node ids after their random
# 0-750 ms delay, while this responder answers in microseconds -- so a stale
# entry here arrives first and wins. This is fine for an experiment and a poor
# basis for anything permanent. See FINDINGS.md.
#
#   node <id> <ipv4>      one physical device.  <id> is its MAC -- the node id is
#                         the cache key a controller uses, so it MUST match the
#                         MAC the real player announces, or the controller will
#                         show the same player twice.  Get it from
#                         `lsdp-static discover` while the players are reachable,
#                         or from the `mac` attribute of
#                         http://<player>:11000/SyncStatus (see from-syncstatus.sh).
#                         `auto` derives a stable made-up id from the address --
#                         fine for a throwaway test, wrong if the real player is
#                         also announcing.
#
#   service <class> k=v.. one advertised service.  Classes: 0x0001 player,
#                         0x0002 server, 0x0003 secondary node in a multi-zone
#                         chassis, 0x0004 sovi-mfg, 0x0005 sovi-keypad,
#                         0x0006 pair slave, 0x0007 remote web UI, 0x0008 hub.
#                         Names also work: player, server, secondary, sovi-mfg,
#                         sovi-keypad, pair-slave, remote-web-ui, hub.
#                         Controllers accept only 0x0001, 0x0003, 0x0006, 0x0008.
#
# TXT keys: `port` (defaults to 11000 if omitted on a player class), `name`,
# `model`, `version`, `zs`.  Controllers read `port` and `version`; `name` and
# `model` let a client show something useful before its first /SyncStatus.
# Unknown keys are ignored by controllers, so extra ones are harmless.

# This is the Bluesound Node N130 announce captured in bluos-http-api.md 12.1;
# `lsdp-static selftest` checks that it encodes to exactly those bytes.
node 90:76:82:42:74:c4 192.168.10.10
  service 0x0001 name="Bluesound Node" port=11000 model=N130 version=3.20.52 zs=0
  service 0x0004 name="Bluesound Node" port=11431

# A second player, minimal: port defaults to 11000.
node 90:76:82:11:22:33 192.168.10.11
  service player name="Kitchen" model=PULSE2i version=3.20.52
